    "name":  "Neonatal jaundice, unspecified",
    "module":  "hepatobiliary",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Neonatal jaundice (neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia) is the most common condition requiring medical assessment in newborns, affecting approximately 60% of term and 80% of preterm infants within the first week of life. It results from an imbalance between bilirubin production (from haem catabolism of fetal haemoglobin) and hepatic conjugation and excretion capacity. Physiological jaundice appears after 24 hours, peaks at day 3–5, and resolves by day 14 in term infants. Pathological jaundice (onset \u003c24h, bilirubin rising rapidly, or persisting beyond 2 weeks) requires urgent investigation to exclude haemolytic disease, infection, metabolic disorders, or biliary atresia.",
